WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.375
Welcome to CSC-244.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:08.379
In this lesson, you’ll choose the operations a Kotlin collection allows, use

3
00:00:08.379 --> 00:00:13.133
lambdas to select and transform its elements, and handle a missing search

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:13.925
result safely.

5
00:00:14.175 --> 00:00:18.325
A lambda is a small function we can pass to another operation.

6
00:00:18.575 --> 00:00:21.625
A collection holds several related values.

7
00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:27.314
These skills turn a group of app records into information a screen can

8
00:00:27.314 --> 00:00:27.675
display.

9
00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:33.058
Separating selection from transformation makes each rule easier to read and

10
00:00:33.058 --> 00:00:33.525
test.

11
00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:38.108
Keeping the source available also lets different parts of an app build

12
00:00:38.108 --> 00:00:40.275
different views from the same records.

13
00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:44.200
Imagine you’re developing an Android notes app.

14
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.225
Each note has a text title and a Boolean archived flag.

15
00:00:48.475 --> 00:00:52.775
Our source has three notes: Lab ideas, Old list, and App sketch.

16
00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:57.525
Old list is archived; the other notes use the default active state.

17
00:00:57.775 --> 00:01:01.200
We also want to search for a title supplied by the app.

18
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.872
Our script will select the active notes, turn those notes into titles, and

19
00:01:06.872 --> 00:01:07.950
print each title.

20
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.921
It will also search the original list and show a helpful message when there is

21
00:01:12.921 --> 00:01:13.550
no match.

22
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.252
We’ll keep selection, transformation, display, and search as separate steps so

23
00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:20.275
their different jobs are clear.

24
00:01:21.376 --> 00:01:24.176
Let’s build this in demo.kts.

25
00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:29.649
The standard Run Code button will execute our script with the installed Kotlin

26
00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:30.051
compiler.

27
00:01:30.303 --> 00:01:34.978
First, describe each note and group the sample notes into an ordered list.

28
00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:40.250
A list fits because we want to keep the source order and process every note in

29
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:40.878
that order.

30
00:01:55.404 --> 00:02:00.501
The familiar Note data class stores a title and an archived flag that defaults

31
00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:01.229
to false.

32
00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:06.254
List of creates a list with read-only operations for our sample values.

33
00:02:06.504 --> 00:02:11.554
Those operations let us read the elements, but do not include add or remove.

34
00:02:11.804 --> 00:02:16.704
A mutable list provides those changing operations when a task needs them.

35
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:21.983
Read-only access is not a promise that every object reachable from a

36
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:23.233
collection is immutable.

37
00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:26.583
Another reference could expose mutable data.

38
00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:32.492
Also, val prevents reassigning a variable; it does not by itself prevent a

39
00:02:32.492 --> 00:02:34.233
mutable collection from changing.

40
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:39.208
Here, our filtering and mapping steps produce separate result lists and leave

41
00:02:39.208 --> 00:02:40.783
the source list intact.

42
00:02:41.043 --> 00:02:45.243
We need a rule that decides which notes belong in the active view.

43
00:02:45.493 --> 00:02:50.100
Filter applies a Boolean test to each source element and keeps the elements

44
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.518
whose test is true.

45
00:02:51.768 --> 00:02:56.693
We’ll pass that test as a lambda, a small function written inside braces.

46
00:03:07.859 --> 00:03:12.134
Inside this lambda, it means the one note currently being tested.

47
00:03:12.384 --> 00:03:17.875
The exclamation mark negates its archived flag, so the test is true for an

48
00:03:17.875 --> 00:03:18.659
unarchived note.

49
00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:21.034
Each note is tested in turn.

50
00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:26.813
The braces follow filter because Kotlin allows a final lambda argument outside

51
00:03:26.813 --> 00:03:27.734
the parentheses.

52
00:03:27.984 --> 00:03:30.284
This is trailing-lambda syntax.

53
00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:35.990
ActiveNotes holds the selected Note objects in source order; filtering has not

54
00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.809
deleted anything from notes.

55
00:03:38.076 --> 00:03:40.301
Work through a different small list.

56
00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:44.101
Suppose Draft is active and Old plan is archived.

57
00:03:44.351 --> 00:03:50.410
Filtering for active notes tests Draft as true and Old plan as false, so the

58
00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.026
result contains Draft only.

59
00:03:52.276 --> 00:03:55.176
The original list still contains both notes.

60
00:03:55.426 --> 00:04:00.746
Selection decides which elements to keep; it does not turn a note into display

61
00:04:00.746 --> 00:04:01.126
text.

62
00:04:01.382 --> 00:04:04.757
The screen needs titles rather than whole Note objects.

63
00:04:05.007 --> 00:04:09.332
Map solves that different problem: it applies a transformation to each

64
00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:13.657
selected element and collects the returned values into a new list.

65
00:04:17.881 --> 00:04:21.606
In this lambda, it refers to one element of activeNotes.

66
00:04:21.856 --> 00:04:26.406
The lambda returns that note’s title, so titles is a list of strings.

67
00:04:26.656 --> 00:04:31.216
With our separate Draft example, mapping the one selected note would produce

68
00:04:31.216 --> 00:04:32.356
the text Draft.

69
00:04:32.606 --> 00:04:36.181
Filter chose a note; map extracted a value from it.

70
00:04:36.431 --> 00:04:39.481
Neither operation changes the original list.

71
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:42.208
Now make those title strings visible.

72
00:04:42.458 --> 00:04:45.408
ForEach performs an action for each element.

73
00:04:45.658 --> 00:04:48.558
We’ll give it a lambda that prints the current title.

74
00:04:48.808 --> 00:04:53.420
Printing is a side effect: it changes what appears in Output instead of

75
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.258
producing a transformed list for us to use.

76
00:04:59.356 --> 00:05:04.135
ForEach visits the strings in titles in order, and println displays each one

77
00:05:04.135 --> 00:05:05.606
on its own line.

78
00:05:05.856 --> 00:05:10.656
Here, it names a string, whereas the earlier lambdas received Note objects.

79
00:05:10.906 --> 00:05:15.306
The collection used for each call determines what that lambda receives.

80
00:05:15.559 --> 00:05:18.884
Finally, a search must allow for an absent title.

81
00:05:19.134 --> 00:05:24.081
Find checks elements until it finds the first match, or returns null when none

82
00:05:24.081 --> 00:05:24.434
match.

83
00:05:24.684 --> 00:05:28.534
We’ll search notes, the complete source list, for the title Missing.

84
00:05:28.784 --> 00:05:34.096
Then we’ll reuse the safe call and Elvis fallback from Lesson Two to choose

85
00:05:34.096 --> 00:05:35.234
display text safely.

86
00:05:43.545 --> 00:05:47.820
The search lambda compares each note’s title with the requested text.

87
00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.945
Match is nullable because a search can fail.

88
00:05:51.195 --> 00:05:57.082
The safe call reads the title only when a note was found, and the Elvis

89
00:05:57.082 --> 00:05:59.045
expression supplies fallback text otherwise.

90
00:05:59.295 --> 00:06:03.445
For a separate example, search a list containing Draft for Budget.

91
00:06:03.695 --> 00:06:06.645
No title matches, so find returns null.

92
00:06:06.895 --> 00:06:10.820
A fallback such as Not found becomes the text to display.

93
00:06:11.072 --> 00:06:15.797
Before running, predict every printed line for our actual three-note list.

94
00:06:16.047 --> 00:06:20.947
Trace which notes pass the archive test, what map returns for them, and what

95
00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:21.997
the search returns.

96
00:06:22.247 --> 00:06:26.997
Then decide whether the final line uses a found title or the fallback.

97
00:06:27.247 --> 00:06:29.322
Pause and write your prediction.

98
00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:39.764
Now run the script and compare the real output with your prediction.

99
00:06:52.102 --> 00:06:57.177
Lab ideas and App sketch appear in source order because both notes are active.

100
00:06:57.427 --> 00:07:02.374
Old list does not appear in that title display because its archive flag is

101
00:07:02.374 --> 00:07:02.727
true.

102
00:07:02.977 --> 00:07:07.972
The last line is No matching note: no source title equals Missing, so the

103
00:07:07.972 --> 00:07:10.827
search returns null and the fallback is used.

104
00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:15.660
We’ve built a display from the source records without removing archived notes

105
00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:16.805
from that source.

106
00:07:17.055 --> 00:07:21.809
Filter selects Note objects, map transforms them into strings, and forEach

107
00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:23.105
prints those strings.

108
00:07:23.355 --> 00:07:28.049
Find answers a different question about the original list, with a nullable

109
00:07:28.049 --> 00:07:30.005
result that we handle safely.

110
00:07:30.255 --> 00:07:34.030
These distinct jobs make the data flow easier to follow.

111
00:07:34.301 --> 00:07:37.476
Now try a reminder list with a completion flag.

112
00:07:37.726 --> 00:07:42.223
Select the unfinished reminders, transform them into display text, and search

113
00:07:42.223 --> 00:07:44.676
for a title that is absent.

114
00:07:44.926 --> 00:07:49.894
Print the source before and after filtering to check that its entries remain

115
00:07:49.894 --> 00:07:50.276
intact.

116
00:07:50.526 --> 00:07:54.689
What evidence would show that your selection, transformation, and

117
00:07:54.689 --> 00:07:57.926
missing-result handling each did the intended job?
